
class Solution {
    public List<List<Integer>> fourSum(int[] nums, int target) {

        // List to store the final answer
        List<List<Integer>> ans = new ArrayList<>();

        int n = nums.length;

        // Step 1: Sort the array
        // Sorting helps us use the two-pointer technique and skip duplicates
        Arrays.sort(nums);

        // Step 2: Fix the first element of the quadruplet
        for(int i = 0; i < n-3; i++){

            // Skip duplicate values for i to avoid duplicate quadruplets
            if(i > 0 && nums[i] == nums[i-1]) continue;

            // Step 3: Fix the second element
            for(int j = i+1; j < n-2; j++){

                // Skip duplicate values for j
                if(j > i+1 && nums[j] == nums[j-1]) continue;

                // Two pointers for remaining two numbers
                int left = j+1;
                int right = n-1;

                // Remaining target after selecting nums[i] and nums[j]
                long remaining = (long)target - nums[i] - nums[j];

                // Step 4: Two-pointer search
                while(left < right){

                    long sum = nums[left] + nums[right];

                    if(sum < remaining){
                        // Need a larger value → move left pointer
                        left++;
                    }
                    else if(sum > remaining){
                        // Need a smaller value → move right pointer
                        right--;
                    }
                    else{
                        // Found a valid quadruplet
                        ans.add(Arrays.asList(nums[i], nums[j], nums[left], nums[right]));

                        // Store current values to skip duplicates
                        int leftVal = nums[left];
                        int rightVal = nums[right];

                        // Skip duplicate values for left pointer
                        while(left < right && nums[left] == leftVal){
                            left++;
                        }

                        // Skip duplicate values for right pointer
                        while(left < right && nums[right] == rightVal){
                            right--;
                        }
                    }
                }
            }
        }

        // Return all unique quadruplets
        return ans;
    }
}

🧠 Logic Summary

Sort the array so duplicates can be handled easily.

Use two nested loops (i and j) to fix the first two numbers.

Use two pointers (left and right) to find the remaining two numbers.

Compare nums[left] + nums[right] with the remaining target.

Skip duplicate elements to avoid repeating the same quadruplet.

⏱ Time Complexity
O(n^3)
📦 Space Complexity
O(1) (excluding result list)
